Garcia Capability Harvest

Mission: Acquire complementary open-source systems, recover their portable mechanisms, map them into Pantheon Prime, preserve provenance, and return both the useful organs and the terrain learned while finding them.

Status: Seven repositories acquired and statically inspected. No third-party code or installation scripts were executed.

Executive Result

Pantheon Prime does not need another generic agent framework. It needs a translation layer that can instantiate its existing protocols across several proven runtimes.

The harvested repositories collectively provide:

distributed agent execution;

temporal graph memory;

optimization of language-model programs;

durable state-machine execution;

long-lived agent memory and learning;

reproducible evaluation;

controlled software action.

Pantheon Prime supplies the missing connective architecture:

intent and invariants;

ThoughtObjects and StateVector;

Garcia route ownership;

NTE Structure/Tension/Resolution;

ZPC conversion of failed routes;

daemons and formations;

Metamap route inheritance;

semantic logistics;

reconstruction across substrates;

and the distinction among mechanism, narrative, operator, evidence, and consequence.

The correct strategy is federated instantiation, not wholesale merging. Pantheon Seals should define portable contracts. Each external project becomes an optional runtime adapter capable of instantiating one or more contracts.

