Evidence Program

Pantheon Prime should prove semantic executability rather than merely assert it.

Experiment A: Repository Bootloader

Give multiple fresh models only the repository and the same real task.

Measure:

time to useful action;

correct component selection;

invariant preservation;

unnecessary clarification requests;

route completion;

quality of Afterglow;

cross-model agreement and productive disagreement.

Experiment B: Reconstruction

Give models progressively smaller Pantheon seeds:

full repository;

front door;

Garcia Packet;

one canonical sentence.

Measure what architecture and behavior can be reconstructed at each compression level.

Experiment C: ZPC

Compare ordinary retry against ZPC-governed retry.

Measure whether failed attempts produce reusable constraints and reduce repeated failure on related tasks.

Experiment D: Runtime Transfer

Implement the same Seal in two runtimes, such as LangGraph and other PantheonOS.

Measure whether invariants and outcomes survive substrate change.

Experiment E: Memory Mesh

Compare transcript-only retrieval, vector retrieval, and Graphiti temporal graph retrieval on long-horizon continuity tasks.

Measure recall, temporal correctness, provenance, contradiction handling, and context efficiency.

Static Security and Provenance Findings

No downloaded project was executed during this harvest.

Observed surfaces requiring quarantine or review before execution:

The other PantheonOS documents a prior compromised-package incident and contains multiple curl | sh installation routes.

Graphiti has published an MCP security fix for a Cypher-injection vulnerability in earlier versions.

Letta includes prepare and postinstall scripts that modify hooks/vendor patches and executable permissions.

OpenHands includes package lifecycle hooks, setup scripts, external installers, runtime services, secrets, and broad tool execution.

Inspect contains a submodule and extensive sandbox/execution infrastructure.

Several repositories document piping remote installers directly to shells.

Pantheon ingestion policy:

pin commit;

identify license;

inspect lifecycle hooks;

inspect submodules;

prohibit remote install piping in automated ingestion;

install only in an isolated disposable environment;

disable telemetry by default;

use least-privilege credentials;

record every imported mechanism and source path;

run deterministic tests before granting a runtime adapter trusted status.

